  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nQuiet weather will continue today and Monday with areas of \nmorning valley and coastal fog. Temperatures will remain near \nseasonable normals. The seabreeze will keep the coast cool \nduring the afternoons. There is a non-zero chance of a few \nshowers or thunderstorms later Monday afternoon and evening \nacross western NH and the western ME mountains. However it \nappears that the forcing for ascent associated with an \napproaching short wave trough will remain far enough west to \nkeep the area mostly dry.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nIt is becoming clear next week will turn wet, especially from \nWednesday onward. A broad upper-level trough will sit over Hudson \nBay. Coming into focus is the likelihood of a cutoff low pressure \nstalling and occluding over the Northeast, bringing at minimum a \nchance for showers and cloud cover for much of next week, but also \nthe potential for locally heavy rainfall coming in a few separate \nwaves.\n\nThe GFS/GEPS has joined the Euro and its ensembles in supporting a \nmore amplified and less progressive low pressure system. However \nspread still remains, with the GFS members clustered over coastal \nNew England, while the Euro members are further north into the \ninterior Northeast. PWATs, while not the most excessive, rise to \n1.50-1.75\", which combined with dew points in the 60s, as well as \npretty strong dynamics (many members deepen this surface low below \n1000 mb), the stage will be set for plenty of heavy downpours. It is \nunclear how much of this precipitation will be stratiform or \nconvective, but noting some MUCAPE on guidance, which would be most \nlikely during the afternoon hours each day if any surface heating \ncan occur. \n\nThe ECMWF ensemble is the most bullish on QPF, while the other \nmembers focus the highest totals to our south. As a result, the \ncurrent NBM spread is very large. The 50th percentile has 1.00-1.75\" \nacross the forecast area, however the 90th percentile indicates 2.50-\n4.00\". This is a robust signal at this timeframe and indicates a \nhigh \"tail\" to the QPF distribution. However, keep in mind this \nrepresents three to four day totals, so while locally heavy rainfall \nis possible, not overly concerned about short-fused flash flooding \nat this time. July has been anomalously dry for some with minor-\nmoderate drought conditions still persisting, so a wet week is \ngenerally good news, as long as the rainfall is not overly excessive.\n\nDespite the increasing humidity, daytime temperatures will likely \ntrend on the cool side, given the increased cloud cover and lowering \n500 mb heights/thickness values by midweek. This would be especially \nlikely if a more southern low pressure track becomes probable.",
